WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:03.631
You have rehearsed this sentence in your head for the fifth time:

2
00:00:04.081 --> 00:00:07.034
‘Team lead, I would rather not take on that project next month.’

3
00:00:07.734 --> 00:00:13.597
You finally say it. Your voice shakes, your face feels hot, and you think the other person sees it all.

4
00:00:14.597 --> 00:00:20.337
Social psychologists call this the illusion of transparency: we tend to overestimate how well others can read our inner state.

5
00:00:21.437 --> 00:00:26.747
In a 2003 experiment, college students worked in pairs and took turns giving an impromptu three-minute speech.

6
00:00:27.147 --> 00:00:32.141
Afterward, most thought they had looked more nervous than their partner.

7
00:00:32.541 --> 00:00:36.124
But their partners, on average, saw less nervousness than they expected.

8
00:00:36.874 --> 00:00:44.066
The researchers’ explanation is that you start from the nerves you feel, then adjust your guess about what others see—but not enough.

9
00:00:44.716 --> 00:00:49.393
That does not mean nervousness stays hidden. Their partners noticed some of it, just less than they thought.

10
00:00:50.143 --> 00:00:58.597
The same paper found that observers rated speakers who had learned about this illusion beforehand as more composed and effective.

11
00:00:58.947 --> 00:01:03.490
Simply telling speakers not to worry about what others thought did not have that effect.

12
00:01:04.040 --> 00:01:08.392
But this was measured immediately in a lab. The authors said they did not know how long it would last.

13
00:01:09.242 --> 00:01:12.929
So the next time your voice shakes, do not immediately assume the other person sees it all.

14
00:01:13.529 --> 00:01:19.220
Would you say, ‘I’m a little nervous,’ or carry on and act as though nothing happened? Share your thoughts.

15
00:01:20.320 --> 00:01:21.602
Say the thing you have been not saying.
